

   =====( TrionTetris V2.00 )=========================================


           Everything you never wanted in a tetris door and MORE !

           (C) Copyright Paul Spijkerman, 1993-1997. All rights reserved.



           V1.00  was made for the Xenolink BBS package.

           V1.40  also ran as a CliDoor.

           V2.00  rewritten for the Trion BBS system.


           This version is especially written for use and
           distribution with the Trion BBS system wich is written and
           copyrigthed by Paul Spijkerman.

           This version will only work on the Trion BBS System !!


   ===================================================================

          (Major) Changes from V1.40 to V2.00

         -Rewritten for Use with the Trion BBS system.

          TrionTetris will use the 'Additional CliDoor Support system'
          to get the UserName and baudrate from the BBS node.


   ===================================================================

          (Major) Changes from V1.20 to V1.40

         -Added possibility to change the game control keys.
          Keys are automatically saved , and resetable with a key.

         -When the game is over you have to Press return , this way
          you only leave the game screen when you want to.

         -The executable is also Useable as a CLIdoor !
          But the XenolinkDoor Mode seems to be a lot faster.

         -The cursor is placed at position 1,1 when playing
          so it isn't attached to the pieces.

         -Enhanced screen clearing.

         -No more can't find file messages in CLI, altough you can
          get them after pressing '!' of '?' in the tetris main menu
          for easy debugging.

         -Joystick is switched off by default in the config file.

         -Added version/date string.


          Some things I totaly forgot about in the last months ;-)

   ===================================================================

          (Major) Changes from V1.00 to V1.20

         -Bug fixed , when someone reached the highest score the
          high score display in the game was not immediatly updated.
          (Thanx to Tom De Ruyter, I never noticed it).

         -Bug fixed with the memory models, so some variabels in an
          array got scrambled.

         -There was a little problem if you didn't had a cfg file.
          (The Big screen got to long).

         -No more fideling around with initiated arrays.
          (Just to keep the code clean and crash free).

         -The code was rewritten for speed , it is much faster now !
          So it runs even beter on unaccelerated machines.

         -Less flickering of pieces being build up and erased.
          (By internal buffering, this way a lot of sprintf
           functions could be eliminated also, even more speed !!)

         -The Speed of the game is configerable.
          If the game was to fast for you, you can slow it down.

         -Better graphics (With some help of Roelof Jansen).

         -The Used field size is mentioned in the Hi-scores.
          (Score files compatible with V1.00)

         -Menus are a little more flexible, you don't have to go to
          the main menu but can give the command immediatly.
          If you don't like it just edit the ANSI files !

         -Tested it on 2 nodes at the same time, worked fine.
          But just in case i have changed the open file routines
          to retry 3 times , for if an other node was saving scores.


   =====( Installing )=============================================

         First you have to make some directorys, and place this
         package in them :

      Doors:TrionTetris/            <- this is where the files go

      Doors:TrionTetris/scores/     <- for the hiscores         (still empty)

      Doors:TrionTetris/Users/      <- for the scores of users  (still empty)


         Make a menu entry , something like this :



      Cmd "T" , 0-255
         CliDoor  "doors:TrionTetris/TrionTetris ~50 "
      EndCmd



   =====( Configuration )=============================================

         You can configure a lot, but it is not necesary.

         The only thing you have to do if you have NTSC or PC users
         is to make the big screen shorter.
         Note that you don't have to use screens of different sizes,
         by editing the .GR1 files and making the screens the same size
         in the config file.

         If you think the game is to fast (at the start) you can increase
         the speed variable in the config file.

         If you have a lot of 2400 baud users that play this door
         you can change the Delays: flag in de config to On.
         The door will then wait after it sends a lot of data.
         (It uses the baudrate to determine how long)
         In this way the game will become more playable.
         Can't figure out which mode works best so just try it.



   =====( Keys )=======================================================

         For some functions there are extra keys defined.

         (They are not mentioned in de Ansi screens)

      In the "main.gr1" they are:

         "H"  in stead of  "1"  for Alltime Hiscores

         "M"  in stead of  "2"  for Monthly Hiscores

         "W"  in stead of  "3"  for Weekly Hiscores

         "Y"  in stead of  "4"  for Your Hiscores

      In the game:

         Enter/(return) to drop a piece  (for convenient 1 hand control)

         "Z" and "C" to turn a piece     (for advanced 2 hand control)



   =====( Levels & Scores )==============================================

         There are 16 levels , after the level 16 the game will just go on.
         Levels change after 500 points.
         The first 5 levels will go faster (and faster).

         After 8 levels the pieces will become more difficult.
         (Configurable , the first number in file tet.dat is the
          number of sets of pieces.
          0 is only the standard set.
          1 is recommended, 2-4 will make it very difficult)

         When you drop a piece one line (key 2) you will get a point.
         Dropping it to the bottom will give you 5 points.

         When a piece reaches the bottom it will give 3 to 6 points,
         (depending on size (= number of components))
         plus the number of the level you are playing.

         When you have full lines you will get bonus points.
         These are 2 times the width of the field ( ~ 20)
         multiplied with the square of the number of lines that
         you cleared.


   =====( Weird things )=================================================

         Weekly scores will be erased at the start of the first game
         after saterday 23.59 Hours.

         When playing the Game remote you can sometimes see thin lines
         on the screen.
         This happens mostly on Terminal programmes in Amiga mode
         or the DLG BBS local mode.
         This happens because the door uses the ANSI BOLD code to
         show 16 colors like on a PC , in the amiga mode only 8 colors
         are shown and the Characters are made BOLD.
         Even the falling pieces are made BOLD. Because these have
         to be erased by a none BOLD space there is a tiny line left.


